<section class="container" style="padding-block:4rem 2rem;max-width:760px;">
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Cobiax is an international supplier of void-former modules for sustainable, concrete-saving building structures. The previous claim, “Betondecken leicht gemacht” (concrete slabs made light), addressed only part of the target group: structural engineers, who can assess the construction method technically — but rarely decide on it alone.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">For developers, lower weight is not an obvious advantage. On the contrary: many assume that heavier buildings are safer and of higher quality. The real advantage of Cobiax slabs lay elsewhere — longer spans at the same weight, and therefore fewer columns in the building.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:760px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The building in the picture above is the argument</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The Elbphilharmonie in Hamburg stands on 1,732 piles in harbour silt. On ground like this, every tonne decides what can be built at all. The floor slabs were built with Cobiax void-former modules; according to the manufacturer, this made it possible to limit the total weight of the building to 200,000 tonnes. The Grand Hall, acoustically decoupled from the rest of the building on steel springs, weighs 12,500 tonnes.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">This is exactly the communication problem this mandate was about: for the structural engineer it is a figure in a calculation. For the developer it is the difference between a design that can be realised and one that cannot. The old claim spoke to the first and left out the second — even though the second decides.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2">Architecture: Herzog &amp; de Meuron. Structural design: WGG Schnetzer Puskas Ingenieure. Construction: Hochtief. Completed 2016.</p>
</section>

<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 3rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">From property to benefit</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The new direction had to convey the target-group-widening advantage directly: more design freedom through fewer columns. This gave rise to the new claim “wider scopes”, which works internationally — deliberately in English, as Cobiax serves international markets, with a focus on the Middle East among others.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">How large this effect is structurally is best shown with German figures — for the Middle Eastern markets there are no surveys comparable to the German chamber and register statistics. The following chart is therefore a documented example of the pattern, not Cobiax's actual market size:</p>

