<section class="container" style="padding-block:1rem 4rem;max-width:900px;">
  <h2 class="display-md reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">Change follows strategy — not the other way round.</h2>
  <p class="body-lg reveal" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">The starting point is not what it should look like, but: what does the direction have to achieve — and does the existing design still carry it?</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-1" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">At Lünstroth a logo is therefore only changed if the strategic analysis shows that the existing form no longer carries the strategy. As a rule it stays unchanged — recognition is an economic asset not to be given up without good reason.</p>
  <p class="body-lg reveal reveal-delay-2" style="margin-bottom:1.5rem;">At Cobiax the change was genuinely necessary: the old logo conveyed neither the actual product advantage nor the new, broader target-group approach. That is why — and only why — it was changed. The characteristic extension of the “x”, the most important element of recognition, was deliberately kept.</p>
</section>

